#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Runs the private-identifier check before anything leaves this machine.
#
# CI runs the same check, and CI is the gate that actually blocks a merge.
# This hook exists because of what CI cannot undo: by the time CI reports a
# private identifier, the commit is already on the forge, and a force-push
# does not remove it. GitHub keeps unreachable objects fetchable by SHA until
# a support request makes them run gc. So the only place a leak is genuinely
# cheap to fix is before the push.
#
# This file is byte-identical in the dev tree and the publication tree. What
# differs between them is scripts/privacy-profile: the dev tree names the two
# paths that are private on purpose (the unscrubbed captured payloads and
# STATE.md), the publication tree allows nothing. Without that file the dev
# tree's gate would be red on every push and would be bypassed permanently,
# which is the same silent-nothing gate wearing a different name.
#
# Enable in a fresh clone (it is per-clone, git does not ship hooks):
#   git config core.hooksPath .githooks
#
# Check it, because a global core.hooksPath in ~/.gitconfig or /etc/gitconfig
# takes precedence for any clone that has no local setting yet, and a clone is
# exactly the case where there is none. See .githooks/commit-msg for the whole
# of it; the short version:
#   git rev-parse --git-path hooks      # must end in .githooks/
#
# Skip once, deliberately, with:
#   AQM_SKIP_PRIVACY_HOOK=1 git push
set -uo pipefail

if [ "${AQM_SKIP_PRIVACY_HOOK:-}" = 1 ]; then
  echo "pre-push: privacy check skipped (AQM_SKIP_PRIVACY_HOOK=1)" >&2
  exit 0
fi

root=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)
if [ ! -f "$root/scripts/ci.sh" ] || [ ! -x "$root/scripts/ci.sh" ]; then
  cat >&2 <<'MSG'
pre-push: BLOCKED. The private-identifier check did not run.

  scripts/ci.sh is missing or not executable, so there is nothing to run and
  nothing was checked. This hook fails closed on purpose. The version it
  replaced exited 0 when scripts/ci.sh was absent, which meant that in the tree
  where commits are actually authored the gate never ran and said nothing — the
  project already writes that failure mode down as "the check existed; nothing
  ran it", and a quiet pass is exactly it.

Fix it, then push again. Two cases, because the file is tracked in one tree
and untracked in the other:

  # tracked (it was ever committed here) -- restores the committed bytes:
  git -C <this repository> checkout -- scripts/ci.sh

  # never committed in this tree -- copy it from its twin, which is byte-identical:
  cp ../ai-quota-meter{,-public}/scripts/ci.sh scripts/ci.sh

  chmod +x scripts/ci.sh

Or say plainly that you want to push without the check:
  AQM_SKIP_PRIVACY_HOOK=1 git push
MSG
  exit 1
fi

if ! "$root/scripts/ci.sh" privacy; then
  cat >&2 <<'MSG'

pre-push: BLOCKED. A private identifier is in the tree or its history.

Fix it before pushing. Once it is on the forge, removing it needs a history
rewrite AND a support request, because a force-push leaves the old blob
fetchable by its SHA.

If this is a false positive, push once with:
  AQM_SKIP_PRIVACY_HOOK=1 git push
MSG
  exit 1
fi
